I worked these out by hand without running any code. All five inputs contain only 0s and 1s, so each hidden pre-activation is the bias b1 plus the W1 weights in the columns where x is 1. Applying relu leaves between 17 and 21 nonzero hidden units per input. Each output is then the sum of those units times the matching W2 weights, plus b2.

Intermediate values were kept to 3 decimals before the final rounding. Small rounding errors could add up across the 32 hidden units, so the second decimal may be off by about 0.01. Three results sit almost exactly halfway between two values: input 3 output 3 (1.765), input 4 output 8 (−3.625) and input 4 output 1 (−2.077).

